## Worked example: swapping a model version on a support agent

Return to the opening scenario with the matrix applied instead of skipped. A team plans to upgrade a support agent from one model version to a newer one, expecting better handling of multi-part questions.

**Contract check.** The team confirms the new model's tool-call syntax and structured-output format match what the agent's downstream code expects — required for a model change, and cheap to verify before anything else.

**Eval suite.** Rather than running only the handful of multi-part-question cases the upgrade was meant to help, the team runs the full representative dataset, because a model change is the one type where an unrelated part of the behavior distribution can move without warning. The full run surfaces something the targeted subset would have missed: on cases involving ambiguous questions, the new model calls the search tool roughly three times as often as the previous version, even though answer quality on those same cases looks slightly improved.

**Staged exposure.** Instead of shipping to all traffic, the team routes a small percentage first, with cost per run and tool-call frequency tracked specifically, not just error rate and eval score.

**Rollback trigger.** The team had already set a cost-per-run threshold before starting — in this hypothetical, 1.5x the baseline — sustained past a defined sample size. The staged sample crosses that threshold within the first exposure window.

**Outcome.** Because the rollback trigger was defined before the release started, the team pauses the ramp immediately rather than debating whether the cost increase is "real" while it burns through a growing share of traffic. They tune the prompt's tool-use instructions to reduce unnecessary search calls, re-run the eval suite, and re-stage. The second attempt clears the cost threshold with the quality improvement intact. The difference between this version of the story and the opening one is not a smarter model or a better prompt — it is a checklist that named a model-version bump as a change requiring full evaluation, staged exposure, and a cost-specific rollback trigger, rather than treating it as a one-line config edit.

## Where this fits with prompt versioning and regression testing

A release checklist is only as useful as the infrastructure underneath it. Staged exposure and rollback both assume the previous version is still addressable and comparable — which is a versioning problem, covered in [prompt versioning and prompt management](/blog/prompt-versioning-and-prompt-management), including how to keep a prompt's version identifier attached to every run so a regression can be traced back to the exact change that caused it. And the eval suite each change type depends on is only as good as the test cases in it, which is why building that dataset from real production signal — not from imagination — is worth treating as its own discipline rather than an afterthought bolted onto the release process.

## Limitations and what this checklist does not cover

This is a release-time checklist, not a substitute for the production-readiness groundwork a first-time launch needs — permission boundaries, redaction, and incident response are covered in the [production checklist](/blog/ai-agent-production-checklist), and this article assumes that groundwork is already in place. It also does not replace judgment: a genuinely trivial change within a category the matrix marks "required" can sometimes be shipped with a lighter check if the team explicitly records why, and a change that looks small but touches a safety-relevant instruction should be treated more like a model change than a prompt change regardless of what the diff line count suggests. The matrix is a default that reduces the chance of forgetting something, not a formula that removes the need to think about the specific change in front of you.

The rollback-trigger thresholds in this article are illustrative examples for a hypothetical workflow, not industry-standard numbers — set your own based on the actual cost, error, and quality baselines your agent has established, and revise them as you learn what a real regression looks like against your own traffic.

## Put this into practice

- Name the change type before writing the change — prompt, model, tool, retrieval, or orchestration — and pull up its row in the matrix, not the previous release's checklist by habit.
- Run the contract check first; an eval score on malformed output is not a meaningful signal.
- Size the eval suite to the change's plausible blast radius, not to how many lines changed.
- Write the rollback trigger and name the owner before exposure starts, not after something looks wrong.
- Close the loop with a short post-release note on what the checks actually caught, so the next release of the same change type benefits from what this one learned.

## Frequently asked questions

{% faq-item question="Do you need a different checklist for a prompt change vs. a model change?" answer="Yes. A model change needs a full re-evaluation since the underlying behavior can shift broadly and unpredictably, while a small, targeted prompt tweak may only need regression cases for the specific scenario it was meant to affect." /%}

{% faq-item question="What is staged exposure?" answer="Releasing a change to a small percentage of traffic before full rollout, so a regression is caught on a small blast radius instead of reaching every user at once." /%}

{% faq-item question="What should trigger an automatic rollback?" answer="A defined threshold breach in error rate, cost per run, or evaluation score, agreed before the release starts — not decided in the moment while a regression is already spreading." /%}

{% faq-item question="Who owns an AI agent release?" answer="Whoever is actively watching monitoring during the exposure window and is empowered to pull the rollback trigger, named before the release starts rather than assumed after something goes wrong." /%}

{% faq-item question="Does every AI agent change need a staged rollout?" answer="No. Low-risk changes with strong eval coverage can sometimes go straight to full traffic, but that decision should be explicit and recorded, not a default from never having thought about it." /%}
